        {
            "number": 4,
            "revision": "4678c18be6de96381d90eae3c528674e7ad892fe",
            "parents": [
                "65074b3cf3a72e2b4e450bfc48b4fb11d9c9a008"
            ],
            "ref": "refs/changes/41/90041/4",
            "uploader": {
                "name": "Nagavarapu Sai Pavan",
                "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                "username": "saipavann27"
            },
            "createdOn": 1778220462,
            "author": {
                "name": "Nagavarapu Sai Pavan",
                "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                "username": "saipavann27"
            },
            "kind": "REWORK",
            "comments": [
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Ravi Teja",
                        "email": "raviteja28031990@gmail.com",
                        "username": "raviteja-b"
                    },
                    "message": "how does community string related to snmp? looks like its IPMI Parameter.\ndo we need to publish this community string in snmp traps as well?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "/COMMIT_MSG",
                    "line": 13,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Nagavarapu Sai Pavan",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "The community string is fundamentally an SNMP v1/v2c concept \u2014 it's the authentication credential in every SNMP packet. PET (Platform Event Trap) is defined as a \"specific format of SNMP Trap used for system management alerting\" (IPMI glossary). The IPMI spec (LAN Parameter 16) states this string is \"Used to fill in the 'Community String' field in a PET Trap.\" So yes, it is published in SNMP traps \u2014 the BMC places this value directly into the SNMP community string field of every PET packet it sends. IPMI just provides the configuration mechanism; the actual consumer is the SNMP protocol stack. That's why the D-Bus interface lives under xyz.openbmc_project.Network.SNMP.\n\n\nRequesting you to check the IPMI 2.0 Doc Table 23-4 and 1-1.Thanks"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Network/SNMP/CommunityString.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Is there any sane default we should use like \"public\"?"
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Network/SNMP/CommunityString.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Nagavarapu Sai Pavan",
                        "email": "saipavann@ami.com",
                        "username": "saipavann27"
                    },
                    "message": "Yes, \"public\" is the standard default per the IPMI specification (Table 23-4, LAN Parameter 16). I've added default: \"public\" to the interface YAML."
                },
                {
                    "file": "yaml/xyz/openbmc_project/Network/SNMP/CommunityString.interface.yaml",
                    "line": 7,
                    "reviewer": {
                        "name": "Patrick Williams",
                        "email": "patrick@stwcx.xyz",
                        "username": "williamspatrick"
                    },
                    "message": "Done"
                }
            ],
